The graph shows the number of downloads $D$ of an app, $t$ weeks after its release. The model is $D=200(2)^{t}$.
Use the equation to find the weekly percentage increase in downloads.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\begin{axis}[width=7.5cm,height=7cm,axis lines=middle,axis line style={-{Stealth}},xmin=-0.3,xmax=3.7,ymin=-150,ymax=1750,xtick={0,1,2,3},ytick={0,200,...,1600},grid=both,clip=false,scaled ticks=false,ticklabel style={font=\small,/pgf/number format/fixed},xlabel={$t$},ylabel={$D$},ylabel near ticks]
\addplot[thick,blue!70!black,->,domain=0:3.1,samples=100] {200*exp(x*ln(2))};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The graph shows the mass $F$ kilograms of fertiliser remaining in a field $t$ weeks after it was spread.
What does the point where the curve meets the vertical axis tell you?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\begin{axis}[width=7.5cm,height=7cm,axis lines=middle,axis line style={-{Stealth}},xmin=-0.6,xmax=8.6,ymin=-8,ymax=100,xtick={0,1,...,8},ytick={0,10,...,90},grid=both,clip=false,scaled ticks=false,ticklabel style={font=\small,/pgf/number format/fixed},xlabel={$t$},ylabel={$F$},ylabel near ticks]
\addplot[thick,blue!70!black,->,domain=0:8.3,samples=100] {90*exp(x*ln(0.8))};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
